indoor and outdoor spaces are critical  and if you don't understand how to  create a good indoor outdoor connection  your project can simply lack what it's  truly trying to achieve what's going on  guys my name is david thomich and  welcome back to the channel if you're  looking for anything architecture  related then you've come to the right  place today we're going to be looking at  a design that connects the indoors to  the outdoors seamlessly it is going to  be a full archicad 25 model as well as a  twin motion export at the end of the day  to be able to produce high-end renders  as you can see to the left-hand side of  me this process has already started and  i'm going to walk you through it step by  step we're going to break down all the  architectural characteristics of this  and what you can take from it if you're  using this in your own personal project  or simply just looking for inspiration  okay so let me talk to you what has  already happened in this design  basically what we've done is we've been  able to try and create some sort of  indoor outdoor connection by also  dividing our public and private spaces  so to be able to achieve this what we've  really done is defined where the public  spaces are and where the private spaces  are our public spaces in my opinion is  anything where anybody will traverse  through your home or somebody will be  able to see you from the street whereas  a private space is something that is a  little bit more intimate something like  a bedroom or a bathroom is very private  and you want to keep that private away  from everybody else then to be able to  connect this back to our nature we're  constantly looking at ways of basically  immersing this design as best as we can  so continuing on with our public private  connection in this stage of the design  what we're looking at doing is creating  definitive spaces inside the ground  floor the ground floor will comprise of  your formal entry or your foyer then  separated with your eating or dining  areas so your dining room and your  kitchen we then move on to a sunken  lounge which then again clearly defines  one space from another without having to  have a major change in the architecture  this way you can clearly differentiate  the psychology of your dining spaces and  the psychology of your living spaces  where you can actually enjoy something a  little bit more or we have to have a  formal meal the best thing about these  spaces being sunken moved shifted and  separated from each other is that we are  able to then clear division around our  backyard if in this instance it was a  rectangular block and we only had four  sides to work with we have the maximum  ability to be able to provide views from  almost all sides nobody really wants to  be looking back towards the street  unless you're looking for some sort of  passive surveillance however we do  really want to be enjoying what we're  experiencing inside of our own design  most people don't have the luxury and  liberty of living on hectares and  hectares worth of land instead they have  a small piece of paradise that they all  use every single day and want to enjoy  every single day when we move upstairs  we really want to move up into those  private spaces a little bit more whilst  still connecting back to our piece of  paradise that we've created in our  backyard by limiting the actual size of  the house we have that ability to  increase the size of our paradise per se  upstairs we're really focusing on our  private spaces our intimate moments so  our bedrooms our bathrooms our small  activity room that might be used for  kids or anybody in the meantime that's  basically just passing through those  spaces without making it too formal in  this case we're using it as a pool table  area which can be changed into anything  that you'd prefer but basically what  we're trying to achieve is two master  bedroom spaces that are continuously  looking outside to our indoor outdoor  connection whilst we also have two  additional bedrooms it can be one it can  be three it the concept is the same  nonetheless that are basically filtering  light so in this instance we are  connecting back to the indoor outdoor  spaces not in a visual connection they  say through to that greenery through to  that water but instead we are connecting  to the light and to the nature so as the  sun passes over this entire project you  can see the light filter and trickle  through the blades into the bedrooms  themselves really creating a soft  delicate space and this can be used as  well when people are potentially  overlooking your site or you're directly  looking at somebody else's neighboring  property this is a great way really to  separate without separating from the  outdoors itself now we always want to  also be able to create some sort of  actual outdoor spaces so balconies are  an incredibly easy incredibly effective  way to do this even if they are a tiny  little juliet balcony off your master  bedroom it is enough to be able to stand  outside get some fresh air and truly  appreciate what you've spent a long time  designing long time creating and most  likely even longer building  when we do get to our backyard and our  little piece of paradise as i like to  call it we really want to focus on  what's important to us so if that means  you truly want a 10 meter pool or a 20  meter pool you want to find out a way of  how you can introduce that into the  design there's nothing worse than having  to come back and go that wasn't actually  what i wanted i wanted a lot more from  that design especially if you had the  ability to do something about it so in  this instance i always try to like to  keep the water as close to the property  as possible because once the water is  close to the property it also allows  that sunlight to basically reflect off  the water and shine onto the actual  property itself reflecting onto the  ceilings of the indoor spaces that you  get to enjoy every single day when  you're at home now when we're actually  looking this at a rendering point of  view we're looking at it from a comfort  point of view we're looking at it from  an actual experiential point of view so  we're looking at how the light travels  over the pathway how it looks from the  street what people will be seeing and  what you'll be experiencing as you come  home to your beautiful luxury bespoke  home now in this instance we again are  trying to connect that indoor outdoor  feeling so as soon as you're coming back  to your house you really want to be  connecting back to nature as much as  possible so to be able to do this what  we've done in this design is basically  broken up the landscaping areas as much  as we can so you're immersed as much as  possible now there might be a house to  the left and right of you but you can  still filter that house away through  planter boxes or even garden beds and in  this instance we've introduced that main  garden bed into the center of this  design which could then also be elevated  further with some garden and some  greenery potentially some grass around  the side but what you want to experience  is when you're coming home you're  experiencing that native emotion you're  experiencing coming back into nature  coming back into your paradise the  actual landscaping that we've selected  in this is very very native to the  program itself it is all very tropical  so we don't have too much control over  the landscaping unless we really want to  see this computer crash and absolutely  have  no final result at the end of the day  but what we've gone for is a tropical  paradise here basically just some really  nice deep greens that are constantly  green throughout the year they're  evergreen plants they're not deciduous  because they don't need to be at such a  low height we're also focusing on the  ability to be able to walk from your car  into your garage into your house through  your front door around the backyard  whichever way you go to be always  connected back to that nature  now i'm a qualified architect i'm not a  qualified landscape architect so i have  no idea about trees and plants and  selections but i do know what does look  good and when we do work with landscape  architects they're there to be able to  actually identify and further bring this  project to reality and to the actual  vision that we're all sharing to be able  to actually come home to something that  is enjoyable now not everybody will have  a porsche out the front of their house  but it is the only high-end quality car  that i have so that's what we're going  with at the moment and nonetheless it is  still being connected back to nature as  much as possible even though that is a  man-made human element it is something  that we want to be taking into  consideration because we don't want to  forget about that at all in the rear of  the property the same concept applies  we're really connecting that water back  to that greenery back to the grass as  much as we possibly can so that no  matter where you stand in the house no  matter where you're looking you're  always connecting back to the greenery  you're always connecting back to the  grass and the water even if it's through  little glimpses of windows or little  pockets of light we always always want  to be trying our best to be able to  connect back to that greenery back to  the outdoors as much as we possibly can  even if that is just a small piece of  fresh air from a balcony or the giant  leaves of a palm tree or a pine tree  simply cantilevering and falling over  the spaces that you're inhabiting at the  moment anyway that's all for me today  guys thank you so much for watching i  hope you enjoyed the video i hope you  learned something new if you did make  sure you smash that like button down  below and don't forget about subscribe  button but like i said that is all from  me so i will see you next monday

